{
    "map": {
        "description": "Creates polygons that cover a given distance from a point, line, or polygon feature.",
        "dissolvetype.descr": "<xdoc><p>Determines how overlapping buffers are processed.</p><ul><li><span title=\"NONE\">No dissolve</span>— Overlapping areas are kept. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"DISSOLVE\">Dissolve overlapping areas</span>— Overlapping areas are combined.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "dissolvetype.domain.DISSOLVE": "Dissolve overlapping areas",
        "dissolvetype.domain.NONE": "No dissolve",
        "dissolvetype.title": "Dissolve Type",
        "distances.descr": "A list of distance values to buffer the input features. You must supply values for either the distances or a distance field. You can enter a single distance value or multiple values. The units of the distance values is supplied by the distance units.",
        "distances.title": "Distances",
        "endtype.descr": "<xdoc><p>The shape of the buffer at the end-of-line input features. This parameter is not valid for polygon input features. At the ends of lines, the buffer can be rounded (round) or be straight across (flat).</p><ul><li><span title=\"ROUND\">Round ends</span>— Buffers will be rounded at the ends of lines. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"FLAT\">Flat ends</span>— Buffers will be flat or straight across at the ends of lines.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "endtype.domain.FLAT": "Flat ends",
        "endtype.domain.ROUND": "Round ends",
        "endtype.title": "End Type",
        "field.descr": "A field from the input layer containing one buffer distance per feature.",
        "field.title": "Distance Field",
        "inputlayer.descr": "The point, line, or polygon features to be buffered.",
        "inputlayer.title": "Input Layer",
        "output.title": "Output",
        "outputname.descr": "The name of the output layer to create on your portal.",
        "outputname.title": "Output Name",
        "params.category1": "Additional Options",
        "ringtype.descr": "<xdoc><p>Determines how multiple-distance buffers are processed.</p><ul><li><span title=\"DISKS\">Concentric overlapping disks</span>— Buffers are concentric and will overlap. For example, if your distances are 10 and 14, the result will be two buffers, one from 0 to 10 and one from 0 to 14. This is the default.</li><li><span title=\"RINGS\">Nonoverlapping rings</span>— Buffers will not overlap. For example, if your distances are 10 and 14, the result will be two buffers, one from 0 to 10 and one from 10 to 14.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "ringtype.domain.DISKS": "Concentric overlapping disks",
        "ringtype.domain.RINGS": "Nonoverlapping rings",
        "ringtype.title": "Buffer Ring Type",
        "sidetype.descr": "<xdoc><p>When buffering line features, you can choose which side of the line to buffer. Typically, you choose both sides (<span class=\"esri_uicontrol\">Full</span>, which is the default). Left and right are determined as if you were walking from the first x, y coordinate of the line (the start coordinate) to the last x,y coordinate of the line (the end coordinate). Choosing left or right usually means you know that your line features were created and stored in a particular direction (for example, upstream or downstream in a river network).</p><p>When buffering polygon features, you can choose whether the buffer includes or excludes the polygon being buffered.</p><p>If a side type is not supplied, the polygon being buffered is included in the result buffer. This is the default for polygon features.</p><ul><li><span title=\"FULL\">Full</span>— Both sides of the line will be buffered. This is the default for line features.</li><li><span title=\"RIGHT\">Right</span>— Only the right side of the line will be buffered.</li><li><span title=\"LEFT\">Left</span>— Only the left side of the line will be buffered.</li><li><span title=\"OUTSIDE\">Outside</span>— When buffering a polygon, the polygon being buffered is excluded in the result buffer.</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "sidetype.domain.FULL": "Full",
        "sidetype.domain.LEFT": "Left",
        "sidetype.domain.OUTSIDE": "Outside",
        "sidetype.domain.RIGHT": "Right",
        "sidetype.title": "Side Type",
        "summary": "<xdoc><p>Creates polygons that cover a given distance from a point, line, or polygon feature.</p><p>Buffers are typically used to create areas that can be further analyzed using a tool such as Overlay Layers. For example, if the question is &quot;What buildings are within 1 mile of the school?&quot;, the answer can be found by creating a 1-mile buffer around the school and overlaying the buffer with the layer containing building footprints. The end result is a layer of those buildings within 1 mile of the school.</p></xdoc>",
        "title": "Create Buffers",
        "units.descr": "<xdoc><p>The units of the buffer distance. You must provide a value if cell size has been set.</p><ul><li><span title=\"MILES\">Miles</span>—Miles</li><li><span title=\"FEET\">Feet</span>—Feet</li><li><span title=\"KILOMETERS\">Kilometers</span>—Kilometers</li><li><span title=\"METERS\">Meters</span>—Meters</li><li><span title=\"NAUTICALMILES\">Nautical miles</span>—Nautical miles</li><li><span title=\"YARDS\">Yards</span>—Yards</li></ul></xdoc>",
        "units.domain.FEET": "Feet",
        "units.domain.KILOMETERS": "Kilometers",
        "units.domain.METERS": "Meters",
        "units.domain.MILES": "Miles",
        "units.domain.NAUTICALMILES": "Nautical miles",
        "units.domain.YARDS": "Yards",
        "units.title": "Distance Units"
    }
}
